### Why not just throw?

In a stream pipeline, throwing on the first bad element aborts the entire pipeline. For batch processing with thousands of records, this means losing all progress because of one malformed entry. The result-object approach processes every element and classifies each as success or failure.

### How do you implement it?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class CollectSuccessesAndErrors {

    record Result<T>(T value, String error) {
        boolean isSuccess() { return error == null; }
        static <T> Result<T> success(T value) { return new Result<>(value, null); }
        static <T> Result<T> failure(String error) { return new Result<>(null, error); }
    }

    static Result<BigDecimal> parseAmount(String raw) {
        try {
            return Result.success(new BigDecimal(raw));
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            return Result.failure("Invalid amount: " + raw);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> rawAmounts = List.of(
            "1500000", "invalid", "900000", "abc", "1200000");

        Map<Boolean, List<Result<BigDecimal>>> partitioned = rawAmounts.stream()
            .map(CollectSuccessesAndErrors::parseAmount)
            .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(Result::isSuccess));

        List<BigDecimal> successes = partitioned.get(true).stream()
            .map(Result::value)
            .toList();

        List<String> errors = partitioned.get(false).stream()
            .map(Result::error)
            .toList();

        System.out.println("Successes: " + successes);
        System.out.println("Errors: " + errors);
        System.out.printf("Processed %d of %d successfully%n",
            successes.size(), rawAmounts.size());
    }
}
```

```text
Successes: [1500000, 900000, 1200000]
Errors: [Invalid amount: invalid, Invalid amount: abc]
Processed 3 of 5 successfully
```

### How does the Result type work?

| Factory | Value | Error | isSuccess |
|---|---|---|---|
| `Result.success(value)` | Present | null | true |
| `Result.failure(error)` | null | Present | false |

### What about using Optional instead of a custom Result?

```java
// Simpler but loses the error message
List<Optional<BigDecimal>> results = rawAmounts.stream()
    .map(s -> {
        try { return Optional.of(new BigDecimal(s)); }
        catch (NumberFormatException e) { return Optional.<BigDecimal>empty(); }
    })
    .toList();

// Keep only successes
List<BigDecimal> values = results.stream()
    .flatMap(Optional::stream)
    .toList();
// Downside: no information about which inputs failed or why
```

### How is the Either/Result pattern different from throwing?

| Aspect | Throwing | Result/Either |
|---|---|---|
| First error | Aborts pipeline | Continues processing |
| Error info | Exception message + stack trace | Stored in result object |
| Partial results | Lost | Preserved |
| Use case | Exceptional, unrecoverable | Expected, per-element |

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: Using exceptions for expected per-element failures in batch processing.

```java
// WRONG: first bad record aborts the entire batch
rawAmounts.stream()
    .map(BigDecimal::new)  // throws NumberFormatException on "invalid"
    .toList();              // aborts, all previous results lost

// CORRECT: result-object approach processes all records
rawAmounts.stream()
    .map(CollectSuccessesAndErrors::parseAmount)
    .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(Result::isSuccess));
```

### What about using vavr's Either or Try?

Libraries like vavr provide production-grade `Either<L, R>` and `Try<T>` types with rich APIs. For production code, consider using them instead of a hand-rolled Result.

### What should a senior engineer know?

This is the functional alternative to exceptions for expected failures. It keeps the pipeline total (every input produces an output) and lets you aggregate errors. The decision between result-objects and exceptions is about whether partial success matters for the task.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "When should you throw vs use Result?" Throw for unrecoverable errors; use Result for expected per-element failures.
- "How do you retry failed records?" Collect the failure inputs and feed them through a retry pipeline.
- "What library provides Either?" vavr (previously javaslang).
